2.11BSD for the 80286: whose each part is, and on what terms

This repository is 2.11BSD's source tree, as the Unix Heritage Society
distributes it, and a port of it to the 286 made on top of it.  Each has
its own terms.


The port's own work

What the port adds is under the BSD 3-clause license in LICENSE,
copyright Jeff Flitton: README.md, docs/, tools/ and tests/, and what it
adds to the tree:

    usr/src/sys/i286, i286at, i286if and i286stand, and usr/src/sys/PC
    usr/src/lib/libc/i286, ccom/i286, c2/i286 and cpp/i286
    usr/src/bin/as/i286 and ld/i286
    usr/src/usr.sbin/timed/i286
    usr/src/local/adduser, bench and ve
    usr/src/GNUmakefile and usr/src/etc/dirs
    usr/include/i286

The port also changes upstream's files.  Those changes are under the
terms of the file each is in.  The branch `upstream` holds the tree as
it was distributed, so `git diff upstream main` marks every change, as
some of the files' notices ask.


The 2.11BSD tree

usr/src and usr/include, but for what the port adds, are 2.11BSD at
patch level 500 (sys is a link to usr/src/sys), from

    https://www.tuhs.org/Archive/Distributions/UCB/2BSD/2.11BSD/

There is no one license for the tree: each file says whose it is and on
what terms.

The University of California's files carry one of Berkeley's notices.
The oldest refer to "the Berkeley software License Agreement"; others
permit redistribution in a paragraph (1988), or in four clauses
(4.4BSD).  On 22 July 1999 the University's Office of Technology
Licensing deleted the third of those clauses, which asked that
advertising acknowledge the University, from every BSD file that has
it, by a notice to all licensees and distributors of BSD
(ftp://ftp.cs.berkeley.edu/pub/4bsd/README.Impt.License.Change).

What comes from AT&T's UNIX, the Seventh Edition and 32V, is under
Caldera International's license of 23 January 2002 for those systems,
which is reproduced at the end of this file from

    https://www.tuhs.org/Archive/Caldera-license.pdf

Files by others carry their own notices, among them sendmail's (Eric
Allman), cron's (Paul Vixie), tcpd's (Wietse Venema), cpp's and two of
stdio's (Anders Magnusson), and identd's (Peter Eriksson).  Some of
2.11BSD's own maintainer's files say they are in the public domain.


Notices that ask more than to be kept

Of the programs this port builds:

    usr/src/usr.bin/patch      may be copied as long as no one makes
                               money off it or claims to have written it
    usr/src/bin/tcsh           its changes to csh may go in a commercial
                               product only if they change nothing of
                               its price or support
    usr/src/usr.sbin/cron      may be distributed freely, and sold with
                               its source, with its author's name kept
                               and changes marked
    usr/src/ucb/tn3270         its documentation is to say that copying
                               and distribution are by permission of the
                               University of California and of Gregory
                               Glenn Minshall, as this does
    usr/src/local/zmodem       may be used freely for non-commercial and
                               educational purposes; commercial or
                               administrative use asks a licence fee for
                               each serial port; it must be passed on as
                               source with its notice
    usr/src/local/ddd          may be used for non-commercial purposes
    usr/src/local/less         may not be sold for profit without its
    usr/src/local/afio         author's consent, nor may afio, cxref or
    usr/src/local/cxref        qterm be sold, and qterm's author must be
    usr/src/local/qterm        credited
    usr/src/games/hack         a copyright line, and nothing more

Of the rest of the tree, which the port does not build, and which is
here as upstream distributes it:

    usr/src/new/rcs            RCS 4: "No part of this software may be
                               sold or distributed in any form or by any
                               means without the prior written
                               permission of the author"
    usr/src/new/kermit5.188    C-Kermit 5A: may not be sold, nor
                               distributed with commercial products or
                               by commercial concerns, without Columbia
                               University's written permission


Not in the repository

The manuals, other systems' sources, the install tape and the
cross-compiler that reference/MANIFEST lists are fetched from where it
says, each under its own terms.  The cross-compiler, Open Watcom, is
installed into toolchain/ from its own distribution, under the Sybase
Open Watcom Public License.


